---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
import { url } from '../utils/paths';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`Research — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="A doctoral research project exploring how mental health professionals can recognise and support growth following psychosis. Led by Sarah Bowles at Salomons Institute, Canterbury Christ Church University."
		/>
		<style>
			.research-hero {
				margin-bottom: 3rem;
				padding-bottom: 2rem;
				border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
			}
			.research-section {
				margin-bottom: 3rem;
				padding-bottom: 3rem;
				border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
			}
			.research-section:last-of-type {
				border-bottom: none;
			}
			.research-questions {
				list-style: none;
				padding: 0;
				margin: 1rem 0 0 0;
			}
			.research-questions li {
				display: flex;
				gap: 1rem;
				margin-bottom: 1rem;
				align-items: flex-start;
			}
			.research-questions .q-num {
				font-weight: 700;
				color: var(--color-accent);
				min-width: 1.5rem;
				flex-shrink: 0;
			}
			.criteria-list {
				list-style: none;
				padding: 0;
				margin: 1rem 0 0 0;
			}
			.criteria-list li {
				display: flex;
				gap: 0.75rem;
				margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
				align-items: flex-start;
			}
			.criteria-list .bullet {
				color: var(--color-accent);
				font-size: 0.6rem;
				margin-top: 0.5rem;
				flex-shrink: 0;
			}
			.related-link-card {
				display: block;
				padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
				border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
				border-radius: var(--radius);
				margin-bottom: 1rem;
				text-decoration: none;
				transition: border-color 0.2s, box-shadow 0.2s;
				background-color: var(--color-bg-offset);
			}
			.related-link-card:hover {
				border-color: var(--color-accent);
				box-shadow: var(--shadow-md);
				text-decoration: none;
			}
			.related-link-title {
				font-weight: 700;
				color: var(--color-text-main);
				display: block;
				margin-bottom: 0.15rem;
			}
			.related-link-subtitle {
				font-size: 0.85rem;
				color: var(--color-accent);
				display: block;
				margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
			}
			.related-link-desc {
				font-size: 0.9rem;
				color: var(--color-text-muted);
				margin: 0;
			}
			.get-in-touch {
				text-align: center;
				padding: 2.5rem 2rem;
				background-color: var(--color-bg-offset);
				border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
				border-radius: 16px;
				margin-top: 3rem;
			}
			.get-in-touch h2 {
				border-bottom: none;
				display: block;
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="research-hero">
				<h1>Growth-Oriented Practice in Psychosis</h1>
				<p style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--color-text-muted); margin: 0;">What helps people grow through and beyond experiences of psychosis?</p>
			</div>

			<section class="research-section">
				<h2>Why this matters</h2>
				<p>Psychosis is frequently traumatic, and the experience can cause significant distress and disruption. A growing body of research, however, shows that people often also experience meaningful growth in its aftermath. This growth can take many forms: a renewed sense of purpose, deeper relationships, a stronger sense of self, new priorities, or a richer spiritual or existential life.</p>
				<p>Despite this body of evidence, mental health services rarely have clear models for how clinicians can recognise and actively support this kind of growth alongside clinical recovery. Without such guidance, clinicians may inadvertently invalidate people's experiences or work with the unrealistic expectation that everyone who experiences psychosis should grow.</p>
				<p>This research aims to begin to fill that gap.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="research-section">
				<h2>What this research asks</h2>
				<p>The study explores four questions, from the perspective of clinicians who actively try to promote growth following psychosis:</p>
				<ol class="research-questions">
					<li><span class="q-num">1.</span><span>How do they understand the ways in which growth can occur following psychosis?</span></li>
					<li><span class="q-num">2.</span><span>How does that understanding shape their clinical practice?</span></li>
					<li><span class="q-num">3.</span><span>What are the core elements of growth-oriented practice, and when should they be applied?</span></li>
					<li><span class="q-num">4.</span><span>What cultural and ethical considerations shape this kind of work?</span></li>
				</ol>
			</section>

			<section class="research-section">
				<h2>A growing evidence base</h2>
				<p>The idea that psychosis can lead to growth is supported by an expanding research literature. A 2018 systematic review synthesised 40 studies of positive change after a first episode of psychosis, finding consistent reports of growth at individual, interpersonal, and spiritual levels (Jordan et al., 2018). These findings are often framed through the lens of post-traumatic growth, the theory that struggling with adversity can lead to positive psychological change including greater appreciation for life, more meaningful relationships, a sense of personal strength, changed priorities, and a stronger spiritual or existential life (Tedeschi &amp; Calhoun, 2004).</p>
				<p>Other theoretical perspectives have also tried to explain growth following psychosis. Jackson's Paradigm Shifting Process Model, for instance, proposes that some psychotic experiences may contain insights that allow a person to resolve an underlying internal conflict, leading to a meaningful shift in how they understand themselves and the world (Jackson, 2010).</p>
				<p>Despite all this work, very little research has asked clinicians themselves how they think about growth following psychosis and how they try to support it. This study is one of the first to do so.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="research-section">
				<h2>What participation involves</h2>
				<p>The study involves a single online interview of 30 to 60 minutes, held over Microsoft Teams at a time convenient for the participant. Sarah asks a range of questions about the participant's experience of working with individuals who have experienced growth following psychosis.</p>
				<p>Interviews are recorded and transcribed via Teams. The transcript is checked for accuracy against the recording and any identifying information is removed, after which the original recording is deleted. Anonymised quotes from interviews may be used in published reports. Participants are not identified in any publication.</p>
				<p>Participants are free to withdraw their data from the study up to two weeks after the interview, without giving any reason.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="research-section">
				<h2>Who can take part</h2>
				<p>Participants are mental health professionals with:</p>
				<ul class="criteria-list">
					<li><span class="bullet">●</span><span>At least one year of experience working with people who have experienced psychosis</span></li>
					<li><span class="bullet">●</span><span>An awareness of the possibility of growth following psychosis</span></li>
					<li><span class="bullet">●</span><span>An active intention to support clients not only to recover from psychosis but also to learn and grow from it</span></li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section class="research-section">
				<h2>Ethical considerations</h2>
				<p>The study has been reviewed and given a favourable opinion by the Salomons Ethics Panel at the Salomons Institute for Applied Psychology, Canterbury Christ Church University (ethics reference ETH2425-0036).</p>
				<p>All information collected is kept strictly confidential. Data is encrypted and stored on password-protected systems on the Canterbury Christ Church University IT network. Only the primary researcher and her supervisors have access. After the project is complete, data is retained in encrypted form for ten years in line with university policy, then destroyed.</p>
				<p>Concerns about any aspect of the study can be raised with Sarah directly at <a href="mailto:sb2144@canterbury.ac.uk">sb2144@canterbury.ac.uk</a>. Concerns that remain unresolved can be raised with her primary supervisor, Dr Anne Cooke (<a href="mailto:anne.cooke@canterbury.ac.uk">anne.cooke@canterbury.ac.uk</a>). Formal complaints can be addressed to Dr Fergal Jones, Clinical Psychology Programme Research Director (<a href="mailto:fergal.jones@canterbury.ac.uk">fergal.jones@canterbury.ac.uk</a>).</p>
			</section>

			<section class="research-section">
				<h2>The research team</h2>
				<p>The study is led by Sarah Bowles, supervised by Dr Anne Cooke (primary supervisor) and Dr Gerald Jordan (co-supervisor). Full biographies are on the <a href={url('/team')}>Team page</a>.</p>
			</section>

			<section class="research-section">
				<h2>Related work</h2>
				<p>This research is part of a wider conversation about positive change following psychosis. The work below offers a starting point for readers who want to explore the field further.</p>

				<a href="https://reach-collective.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="related-link-card">
					<span class="related-link-title">The Reach Collective</span>
					<span class="related-link-subtitle">University of Bristol</span>
					<p class="related-link-desc">Dr Gerald Jordan's research group, examining post-traumatic growth following psychosis and related areas of recovery and citizenship.</p>
				</a>

				<a href="https://www.bps.org.uk/guideline/understanding-psychosis-and-schizophrenia" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="related-link-card">
					<span class="related-link-title">Understanding Psychosis and Schizophrenia</span>
					<span class="related-link-subtitle">British Psychological Society</span>
					<p class="related-link-desc">The landmark BPS public information report on psychosis, edited by Dr Anne Cooke. Written for service users, families, journalists, policymakers, and mental health workers.</p>
				</a>

				<a href="https://www.researchintorecovery.com/research/epoch/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="related-link-card">
					<span class="related-link-title">EPOCH</span>
					<span class="related-link-subtitle">University of Nottingham</span>
					<p class="related-link-desc">The Experiences of Personal Outcomes from Hearing about recovery study, part of the Research into Recovery group's work on narrative-based approaches to recovery.</p>
				</a>

				<a href="https://blogs.canterbury.ac.uk/discursive/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="related-link-card">
					<span class="related-link-title">Discursive of Tunbridge Wells</span>
					<span class="related-link-subtitle">Salomons blog</span>
					<p class="related-link-desc">The Salomons blog, with regular writing on critical and psychological approaches to mental health, edited by Anne Cooke and colleagues.</p>
				</a>
			</section>

			<div class="get-in-touch">
				<h2>Get in touch</h2>
				<p>If this work resonates with you and you'd like to discuss it or contribute, please get in touch.</p>
				<a href="mailto:sb2144@canterbury.ac.uk" style="font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: 600; font-family: var(--font-serif);">
					sb2144@canterbury.ac.uk
				</a>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
